@@ -0,0 +1,148 @@
+# Accuracy from audio, and the default speech model
+
+`evals/` measures the sorter on clean text. This folder measures the whole path a
+dictated note takes: speech, then Whisper, then the sorter. It answers two questions:
+how many words does each speech model get wrong, and how many of the 59 cases still
+come out right when the sorter only sees what Whisper heard.
+
+Run on 2026-11-20 on a 4-core CPU box, no GPU, in the project venv (not Docker).
+
+## What was run
+
+1. Each of the 59 case transcripts in `evals/capture_cases.jsonl` was turned into
+   speech with gTTS (Google text-to-speech, English voice, mp3). Clips are not in the
+   repo (about 2 MB; regenerate with `gTTS(text, lang="en")`).
+2. Each clip was POSTed to `/transcribe?log=false&language=auto` as raw
+   `application/octet-stream`, one at a time, the way the iPhone Shortcut posts.
+   Seconds are wall clock per request, memory is the server process RSS.
+3. Word error rate against the source text (stdlib, lowercase, punctuation stripped).
+   "Raw" compares words as written. "Numbers folded" turns number words into digits on
+   both sides ("fourteen" = "14", "one hundred and fifty" = "150").
+4. Each recovered transcript went through the real `CaptureService._classify`
+   (Gemini `gemini-3.1-flash-lite`, free tier, one call at a time about 5 s apart) with
+   that case's own "today", and was scored by `evals/score.py` against the unchanged
+   expected answers. Every run answered 59 of 59 cases once Gemini's transient errors
+   were re-run (see the table notes).
+
+Reproduce (server running with the model you want):
+
+    python evals/audio/run_audio.py transcribe --clips CLIPS --out t.jsonl --server-pid PID
+    python evals/audio/run_audio.py wer --transcripts t.jsonl
+    python evals/audio/run_audio.py sort --transcripts t.jsonl --out p.jsonl
+    python evals/audio/run_audio.py merge --transcripts t.jsonl --preds p.jsonl --out results.jsonl
+    python -m pytest evals/audio -c /dev/null          # runner tests, network stubbed
+
+## Numbers
+
+| | breeze-asr-25 (default) | large-v3-turbo |
+|---|---|---|
+| WER, raw | 14.0% (92 of 657 words) | 10.7% (70 of 657) |
+| WER, numbers folded | 10.6% (69 of 650) | 7.5% (49 of 650) |
+| Clips transcribed word for word (folded) | 36 of 59 | 38 of 59 |
+| End to end correct, run 1 | 53 of 59 | 49 of 59 |
+| End to end correct, run 2 (same transcripts, sorted again) | 54 of 59 | 50 of 59 |
+| Answered (sorter returned, no error) | 59 of 59 | 59 of 59 |
+| Seconds per clip, mean (median, max) | 12.0 (11.6, 16.9) | 15.6 (15.4, 23.5) |
+| Server memory, after load / after 59 clips | 2.4 GB / 3.0 GB | 4.0 GB / 4.1 GB |
+| Model on disk | 1.5 GB | 1.6 GB |
+
+Stored run on clean text (2026-11-12, same sorter): 57 of 59.
+
+Notes on the table:
+
+- Run 1 for breeze first read 52 of 59: two cases failed with a Gemini 503 "high demand"
+  error, not a wrong answer. They were sorted again (`--retry-errors-of`) and the rest kept
+  as they were. Run 2 had one classify timeout, handled the same way. Turbo had none.
+- Seconds: the 10 clips re-timed on a quiet machine gave 12.4 s (breeze) and 15.1 s (turbo),
+  the same as the full run. Clips are 2 to 8 seconds long; `language=auto` adds a detect pass.
+- The Malay-tagged cases (6, words like "esok", "lori", "bayar", "seratus") score far worse
+  than the rest: breeze 42.4% (28 of 66 words) against 7.0% on the other 53; turbo 36.4%
+  against 4.3%.
+- A WER of zero is not the goal in four cases: the `stt-misheard-*` sources contain the
+  mishearing on purpose ("ring it", "wiser"); a model that writes "ringgit" is scored wrong.
+
+## Which cases flip, and is it hearing or sorting
+
+Against the stored clean run (57): breeze lost 4 and gained 0; turbo lost 9 and gained 1.
+To tell hearing from sorting, the clean text of the 11 cases that lost in either model
+was sorted three more times:
+
+- Pass on clean text 3 of 3 times, fail from audio: the loss is hearing (or how Whisper
+  wrote the words). 8 cases.
+- Fail on clean text some of the time: the loss is the sorter's own noise. 3 cases:
+  `multi-three-kinds` (2 of 3), `ambig-lorry-which-project` (2 of 3),
+  `ambig-balance-due` (0 of 3 today, though the stored run passed it).
+
+| Case | breeze | turbo | Cause |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| amount-malay-words | lost | lost | hearing: "seratus lima puluh" heard as "Saratis Lima pulo", sorted as 50 (breeze) and 350 (turbo), not 150 |
+| malay-lori-note | lost | lost | hearing: "cakap" heard as "kakup"; sorted as a task with a due date, not a note |
+| multi-manglish-run-on | lost | lost | hearing: "tol" heard as "toddy" or "tall"; breeze filed it as "Parking fee" |
+| both-malay-lori | kept | lost | hearing: "esok" heard as "isaq", due date dropped |
+| malay-esok-klinik | kept | lost | hearing: "esok" heard as "Isak", due date wrong (2026-11-16, not 11-17) |
+| multi-two-dated-tasks | kept | lost | hearing: Whisper wrote "loan Friday. Send the form", so the form task lost its Friday |
+| quote-motorcycle | kept | lost | scorer artifact: "Y-15" does not contain the keyword "y15"; the sorted note was fine |
+| multi-three-kinds | lost run 1, kept run 2 | kept | sorter noise (due date invented for "buy milk"); the audio differs only by "Dr" vs "Doctor" |
+| ambig-balance-due | kept | lost run 1, kept run 2 | sorter noise |
+| date-year-rollover | kept | lost run 1, kept run 2 | sorter noise: due 2026-01-03, a year off |
+| ambig-lorry-which-project | lost | kept run 1, lost run 2 | also a stored clean-run miss; "lorry" heard as "Laurie" or "Lori" |
+
+`multi-five-across-topics` fails from audio in every run of both models. It is also one of the
+two misses in the stored clean run (a five-item note sorted as four), as is
+`ambig-lorry-which-project`.
+
+## Ten most damaging mishearings
+
+Damage means it changed a field the scorer checks, or would have changed what is filed.
+
+| Said | breeze heard | turbo heard | Effect |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| seratus lima puluh ringgit | Saratis Lima pulo ringat | Serratis Lima Pulo Ringgit | amount 50 / 350 instead of 150 |
+| bayar tol 12 ringgit | A toddy by are tall twelve ring it | A toddy buy our tall 12 ringgit | "tol" lost; breeze filed "Parking fee" |
+| boss cakap lori sampai | Boss kakup lori sampai | Boss Kakup Lori Sampai | note turned into a task with a due date |
+| esok pagi kena pergi klinik | Esak Pagi Kena Perji Clinic | Isak Pagi Kenna Pergi Clinic | turbo: due date wrong |
+| dah bayar ... esok kena confirm | isak kena confirm | isaq kenna confirm | turbo: due date dropped |
+| Friday send the signed form | Friday send the signed form | Friday. Send the signed form | turbo: Friday attached to the wrong task |
+| Honda RS150 | Honda 150 rupees | Honda 150 rupees | a currency word invented (the sorter ignored it) |
+| Movers confirmed ... Paid them 200 deposit | ok updatemovers confirmed ... itpaid them | correct | breeze: words run together, no spaces |
+| lorry is booked | Laurie is booked | Lori is booked | keyword check fails |
+| RM85 / Y15 / 9800 | RM 85 | RM 85 / Y-15 / 9,800 | formatting only, but trips crude keyword checks |
+
+## Recommendation
+
+Keep `breeze-asr-25` as the default; the registry default is unchanged.
+
+Turbo hears English better (7.5% against 10.6% numbers folded) but that did not turn into
+better filing: it was 4 cases behind in both end-to-end runs (49 and 50 against 53 and 54),
+took 30% longer per clip (15.6 s against 12.0 s on this CPU) and held 1.6 GB more memory.
+Four of its extra losses (both-malay-lori, malay-esok-klinik, multi-two-dated-tasks,
+quote-motorcycle) failed in both runs. The evidence does not show turbo winning anywhere
+that matters for this speaker, and it costs more, so there is no reason to switch.
+
+This is not proof that breeze is better. The gap is 4 cases of 59 and the sorter alone moves
+a case or two between runs. `large-v3-turbo` stays available (`MODEL_NAME=large-v3-turbo`)
+now that its registry entry points at a repo that exists.
+
+## Limits. Read before quoting a number.
+
+- **n is 59 cases, 657 reference words.** Per-group numbers (6 Malay cases, 66 words) are
+  hints. Below n = 300 do not read any of this as an error rate.
+- **The cases were written by a model.** The expected answers are one reading of the prompt.
+- **One synthetic voice.** gTTS has one clean American-English voice, no accent, no phone
+  microphone, no background noise, no hesitation. Real speech will do worse than these WERs.
+- **Malay words are read by an English voice.** gTTS (lang "en") says "esok" and "seratus" with
+  English sounds, so the 6 Malay cases test how models handle mispronounced Malay, not a
+  Malaysian speaker saying it. The Malay WER (42% and 36%) says nothing about which model is
+  better for a real Manglish speaker. The ranking on Malay is not measured.
+- **Sorter noise.** The sorter is not deterministic. Sorting the same transcripts a second time
+  changed the verdict of 1 case (breeze) and 3 cases (turbo); the clean-text re-sort moved 3 of 11. Differences
+  of one or two cases are noise.
+- **Timing is one 4-core CPU, `int8_float32`** (the CPU does not support `int8_float16`; ctranslate2
+  converts at load, see the server log). A GPU, or a different CPU, changes the ratio.
+- **Memory is the server process RSS** read from `/proc`, not a peak profile.
+- **Keyword checks are crude** (see `evals/README.md`); `quote-motorcycle` is lost to one.
+- **Only English was passed as `language=auto`.** The Shortcut does the same; setting
+  `language=ms` or `en` was not tried.
+
+Not measured: Claude as the sorter, a human voice, a phone microphone, a Windows host,
+`language=` hints, quantisations other than the registry defaults.
